course: dictionary
lesson: 2332
title: Cruel and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Cruel» describes someone who hurts people or animals on purpose, or who does not care when they suffer. Look at the words around it to check. If someone laughs at a person who is crying, the clue is the crying: the person is being cruel.
explain: «Rumours about where a family's money comes from can be cruel; say the truth calmly and tell a teacher if it keeps going.» Here cruel means unkind and hurtful. Try swapping in your meaning: "Rumours can be unkind and hurtful." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Sometimes «cruel» describes a situation, not a person. «It was cruel luck that the picnic was rained off.» Nobody is being unkind here. The luck was very hard to bear, and that is the second meaning of cruel.
fact: «Cruel» means causing pain or suffering to people or animals on purpose, or not caring when they suffer.
fact: «Cruel» can also mean very hard to bear, as in «cruel weather» or «cruel luck».
fact: The noun is «cruelty», as in «cruelty to animals», which means harming animals on purpose.
fact: Phrases to know: «be cruel to» someone, and «a cruel prank», which is a mean trick.
fact: If someone is being cruel to you, tell a trusted adult, such as a teacher or a parent.
exercise: «Aroha forgave a friend for a cruel prank, but asked her to say sorry to the whole class too.» What does cruel mean here? | Quiet and shy | Clever and surprising | *Unkind, causing hurt feelings on purpose | Loud and noisy
exercise: «Cutting a worm in half usually kills it, and it is cruel to try.» What does cruel mean in this sentence? | Very cold | Easy and quick | *Causing pain or suffering on purpose | Very funny
exercise: Which of these is an example of cruelty to animals? | Brushing a pony's coat | Taking a dog for a walk in the park | Feeding a stray cat every day | *Leaving a hungry dog without food on purpose
test: «Friends don't have to agree on everything; disagreeing without being cruel can make a friendship stronger.» What does cruel mean here? | *Being unkind on purpose, hurting a friend's feelings | Being very loud | Changing your mind quickly | Telling a joke
test: Ella says, «It's cruel luck that the picnic was rained off.» What does cruel suggest? | *Something very hard to bear | A long, quiet walk | A funny story | A lucky, happy day
exam: «Rumours about where a family's money comes from can be cruel.» What is the best thing to do if someone keeps spreading cruel rumours? | Laugh and join in | Make up a bigger rumour | Repeat the rumour to your friends | *Say the truth calmly and tell a teacher if it keeps going
